        "---\n",
        "title: \"The Transformer Architecture\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Residual nonlinear operators on token sequences\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "The Transformer combines the self-attention mechanism from Chapter 14 with residual connections, normalization, and position-wise neural networks.  From a mathematical point of view, a Transformer layer is a learned nonlinear map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F:R^{T\\times d}\\to R^{T\\times d}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "that transforms a matrix of token representations into a new matrix of contextual token representations.  The key architectural idea is not a single formula, but a carefully organized composition of simple maps:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{linear maps}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\text{softmax attention}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\text{residual addition}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\text{normalization}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\text{position-wise nonlinear maps}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This chapter studies the Transformer as an operator on sequence matrices.  We emphasize dimensions, probability models, optimization objectives, and why the architecture is easier to train than a plain deep stack of nonlinear maps.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. describe a Transformer layer as a map from $R^{T\\times d}$ to $R^{T\\times d}$;\n",
        "2. distinguish encoder blocks, decoder blocks, and encoder-decoder blocks;\n",
        "3. write the formulas for multi-head attention, residual connections, layer normalization, and feedforward sublayers;\n",
        "4. explain why residual connections help gradient flow;\n",
        "5. explain layer normalization as a row-wise standardization map;\n",
        "6. compute the parameter count of a basic Transformer block;\n",
        "7. connect decoder-only Transformers with autoregressive language modeling;\n",
        "8. implement a small Transformer block from scratch in NumPy;\n",
        "9. use a Plotly.js interactive to visualize how attention, residual mixing, and nonlinear feedforward maps change token representations;\n",
        "10. describe the main mathematical difference between RNNs and Transformers.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.1 The sequence-matrix viewpoint\n",
        "\n",
        "Let a token sequence have length $T$.  After token embedding and positional encoding, the input to a Transformer layer is a matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\in R^{T\\times d},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where row $x_i^T\\in R^d$ represents token position $i$.  The Transformer layer produces another matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y=F(X)\\in R^{T\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The row $y_i^T$ is a contextual representation of token position $i$.  The same layer is applied to every example in a batch, but for mathematical clarity we first ignore the batch dimension.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple Transformer encoder layer has the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "H &= \\operatorname{LayerNorm}\\bigl(X + \\operatorname{MHA}(X)\\bigr),\\\\\n",
        "Y &= \\operatorname{LayerNorm}\\bigl(H + \\operatorname{FFN}(H)\\bigr).\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is called a **post-norm** block because layer normalization is applied after the residual addition.  Many modern implementations use a **pre-norm** block:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "H &= X + \\operatorname{MHA}(\\operatorname{LayerNorm}(X)),\\\\\n",
        "Y &= H + \\operatorname{FFN}(\\operatorname{LayerNorm}(H)).\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The two designs use the same basic pieces, but place normalization in different locations.  Pre-norm blocks are often easier to train in very deep networks because the residual path is more direct.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Main mathematical abstraction.**  A Transformer is a deep composition\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_L\\circ F_{L-1}\\circ\\cdots\\circ F_1\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "of sequence-to-sequence maps, where each $F_\\ell$ mixes token positions by attention and transforms feature coordinates by feedforward nonlinear maps.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.2 Multi-head attention inside a Transformer block\n",
        "\n",
        "A Transformer does not usually use a single attention head.  It uses $h$ heads in parallel.  Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\in R^{T\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For head $r=1,\\ldots,h$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q_r=XW_Q^{(r)},\\qquad\n",
        "K_r=XW_K^{(r)},\\qquad\n",
        "V_r=XW_V^{(r)},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_Q^{(r)},W_K^{(r)}\\in R^{d\\times d_k},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "W_V^{(r)}\\in R^{d\\times d_v}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The $r$th head output is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Z_r=\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}\\left(\\frac{Q_rK_r^T+M}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V_r\n",
        "\\in R^{T\\times d_v}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The matrix $M\\in R^{T\\times T}$ is optional.  For encoder self-attention, $M$ may be a padding mask.  For decoder self-attention, $M$ also includes a causal mask.\n",
        "\n",
        "The head outputs are concatenated:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Z=\\operatorname{concat}(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_h)\\in R^{T\\times hd_v}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then a final output projection gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{MHA}(X)=ZW_O,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "W_O\\in R^{hd_v\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A common choice is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_k=d_v=d/h,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "so $hd_v=d$ and the output has the same feature dimension as $X$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why multiple heads?\n",
        "\n",
        "A single attention head has one learned geometry for comparing tokens.  Multiple heads allow several comparison geometries in parallel.  One head might emphasize local syntactic relations, another might emphasize long-range dependencies, and another might attend to special tokens or punctuation.  This interpretation should be used carefully: attention heads are learned numerical objects, not guaranteed linguistic modules.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, multi-head attention increases expressiveness by applying several different bilinear score functions\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(x_i,x_j)\\mapsto x_i^TW_Q^{(r)}(W_K^{(r)})^Tx_j\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and several different value transformations\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_j\\mapsto x_j^TW_V^{(r)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus each head defines a different kernel-like similarity and a different message representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.3 Residual connections\n",
        "\n",
        "A residual connection adds the input of a sublayer to its output.  If $G:R^{T\\times d}\\to R^{T\\times d}$ is a sublayer, the residual map is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "R(X)=X+G(X).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The derivative of this map has the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "DR(X)=I+DG(X),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $I$ denotes the identity map on $R^{T\\times d}$.  This is the main mathematical reason residual connections help optimization: even if $DG(X)$ has small singular values, the identity term provides a direct path for gradients.\n",
        "\n",
        "To see the idea in a scalar example, suppose\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "y=x+g(x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{dy}{dx}=1+g'(x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In a deep non-residual composition, derivatives multiply many factors.  In a residual network, each layer has an identity contribution, so information and gradients can pass through the network more easily.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Residual learning.**  A residual block does not need to learn the entire transformation.  It only needs to learn a correction to the identity map.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.4 Layer normalization\n",
        "\n",
        "Layer normalization standardizes each token vector across its feature coordinates.  For a row vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_d)\\in R^d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mu(x)=\\frac{1}{d}\\sum_{j=1}^d x_j,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\sigma^2(x)=\\frac{1}{d}\\sum_{j=1}^d (x_j-\\mu(x))^2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Layer normalization maps $x$ to\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{LN}(x)_j\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\gamma_j\\frac{x_j-\\mu(x)}{\\sqrt{\\sigma^2(x)+\\varepsilon}}+\\beta_j,\n",
        "\\qquad j=1,\\ldots,d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\gamma,\\beta\\in R^d$ are trainable parameters and $\\varepsilon>0$ is a small constant for numerical stability.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a sequence matrix $X\\in R^{T\\times d}$, layer normalization is applied row by row:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{LN}(X)_{i,:}=\\operatorname{LN}(X_{i,:}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Layer normalization is different from batch normalization.  It does not compute statistics across examples in a batch.  Instead, it computes statistics across the coordinates of one token representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Geometric interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "Ignoring $\\gamma$ and $\\beta$, layer normalization subtracts the component in the direction of the all-ones vector and rescales to approximately fixed length.  If\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\ldots,1)^T\\in R^d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then centering replaces $x$ by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x-\\mu(x)\\mathbf{1}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This vector lies in the hyperplane\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\{z\\in R^d: z^T\\mathbf{1}=0\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus layer normalization controls scale and location of token features, making the optimization problem more stable.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.5 Position-wise feedforward networks\n",
        "\n",
        "After attention mixes information across token positions, a Transformer block applies the same feedforward network to each token position independently.  For one row $h_i\\in R^d$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{FFN}(h_i)=\\sigma(h_iW_1+b_1)W_2+b_2,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_1\\in R^{d\\times d_{\\mathrm{ff}}},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "W_2\\in R^{d_{\\mathrm{ff}}\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The activation $\\sigma$ is applied coordinatewise.  Common choices include ReLU and GELU.  In matrix form,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{FFN}(H)=\\sigma(HW_1+\\mathbf{1}b_1^T)W_2+\\mathbf{1}b_2^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This sublayer does not mix token positions.  It transforms the feature representation at each position.  Attention mixes positions; the feedforward network mixes coordinates.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Two kinds of mixing.**  In a Transformer block, attention mixes rows of $X$, while the feedforward network mixes columns of $X$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.6 Encoder blocks\n",
        "\n",
        "A Transformer encoder is designed to read an entire input sequence and produce contextual representations for all positions.  An encoder block uses bidirectional self-attention: every non-padding token may attend to every other non-padding token.\n",
        "\n",
        "For an encoder block, a typical post-norm formula is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "H &= \\operatorname{LN}(X+\\operatorname{MHA}_{\\text{enc}}(X)),\\\\\n",
        "Y &= \\operatorname{LN}(H+\\operatorname{FFN}(H)).\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The attention mask usually prevents real tokens from attending to padding positions.  It does not prevent a token from attending to future positions because the encoder is not generating left-to-right.\n",
        "\n",
        "Encoder-only architectures are natural for:\n",
        "\n",
        "- document classification;\n",
        "- token classification;\n",
        "- sentence similarity;\n",
        "- extractive question answering;\n",
        "- sequence representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, an encoder is a map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_{\\text{enc}}:R^{T\\times d}\\to R^{T\\times d}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "that is allowed to use information from the full input sequence at every output position.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.7 Decoder blocks\n",
        "\n",
        "A Transformer decoder is designed for generation.  In a decoder-only language model, token position $t$ should not use information from positions $t+1,t+2,\\ldots,T$ when predicting the next token.  This is enforced by a causal mask.\n",
        "\n",
        "For decoder self-attention, the mask satisfies\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "M_{ij}=\\begin{cases}\n",
        "0, & j\\le i,\\\\\n",
        "-\\infty, & j>i.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then after applying row-wise softmax, all future-position weights are zero:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "a_{ij}=0\\qquad\\text{for }j>i.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A decoder-only block has the same two main sublayers as an encoder block, but with causal self-attention:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "H &= \\operatorname{LN}(X+\\operatorname{MHA}_{\\text{causal}}(X)),\\\\\n",
        "Y &= \\operatorname{LN}(H+\\operatorname{FFN}(H)).\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Decoder-only architectures are natural for autoregressive language modeling:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_1,\\ldots,w_T)=\\prod_{t=1}^T P(w_t\\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The model predicts a probability distribution for the next token at each position.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.8 Encoder-decoder Transformers\n",
        "\n",
        "The original Transformer architecture for machine translation used both an encoder and a decoder.  The encoder reads the source sequence.  The decoder generates the target sequence.  The decoder uses two kinds of attention:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **causal self-attention** over previously generated target tokens;\n",
        "2. **cross-attention** from target positions to source positions.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X_{\\text{src}}\\in R^{T_s\\times d},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "X_{\\text{tgt}}\\in R^{T_t\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The encoder produces\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "E=F_{\\text{enc}}(X_{\\text{src}})\\in R^{T_s\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In cross-attention, queries come from the decoder state, while keys and values come from the encoder output:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Q=HW_Q,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "K=EW_K,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "V=EW_V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The cross-attention output is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{CrossAttn}(H,E)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}_{\\text{row}}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This lets each target position decide which source positions are relevant.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.9 Output layer and language modeling\n",
        "\n",
        "For a decoder-only language model, the final Transformer representation at position $t$ is a vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t\\in R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A linear output layer maps $h_t$ to vocabulary logits:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_t=h_tW_U+b_U\\in R^{|V|},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_U\\in R^{d\\times |V|},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "b_U\\in R^{|V|}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The conditional distribution over the next token is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P_\\theta(w_{t+1}=v\\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_t)\n",
        "=\\frac{\\exp(z_{t,v})}{\\sum_{u\\in V}\\exp(z_{t,u})}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a training sequence $w_1,\\ldots,w_T$, the negative log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal{L}(\\theta)\n",
        "= -\\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\\log P_\\theta(w_{t+1}\\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Dividing by $T-1$ gives the average cross-entropy loss.  Exponentiating average cross-entropy gives perplexity.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.10 Parameter count of a basic block\n",
        "\n",
        "Assume a Transformer block has model dimension $d$, number of heads $h$, and feedforward dimension $d_{\\mathrm{ff}}$.  Suppose each head uses\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_k=d_v=d/h.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The query, key, and value projections across all heads together have approximately\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "3d^2\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "weights.  The output projection has\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d^2\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "weights.  Therefore multi-head attention has about\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "4d^2\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "weights, ignoring biases.\n",
        "\n",
        "The feedforward sublayer has\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "dd_{\\mathrm{ff}}+d_{\\mathrm{ff}}d=2dd_{\\mathrm{ff}}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "weights, ignoring biases.  Layer normalization contributes $2d$ parameters per normalization layer: scale $\\gamma$ and shift $\\beta$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus a rough block parameter count is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "4d^2+2dd_{\\mathrm{ff}}+4d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "for a block with two layer normalization modules and no bias counting in the linear layers.\n",
        "\n",
        "A common choice is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\mathrm{ff}}=4d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then the feedforward sublayer has about $8d^2$ weights, which is often more than the attention sublayer.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.11 Computational complexity\n",
        "\n",
        "Self-attention forms the score matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S=QK^T\\in R^{T\\times T}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Computing $S$ costs on the order of\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "O(T^2d)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "operations.  Multiplying the attention matrix by the values also costs about\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "O(T^2d).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The feedforward sublayer costs\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "O(Tdd_{\\mathrm{ff}}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus the cost of attention grows quadratically with sequence length $T$.  This is the main computational bottleneck for long-context Transformers.\n",
        "\n",
        "RNNs process sequences sequentially with cost roughly linear in $T$, but they are hard to parallelize over time.  Transformers use $T^2$ pairwise comparisons, but these comparisons can be computed in parallel on modern hardware.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Tradeoff.**  RNNs have sequential recurrence.  Transformers replace recurrence with parallel pairwise interaction.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 15.12 Interactive: Transformer block geometry explorer\n",
        "\n",
        "The following interactive uses a two-dimensional toy sequence.  It is not a real Transformer model, but it visualizes three structural ideas:\n",
        "\n",
        "- attention mixes token positions;\n",
        "- residual connections pull the transformed representation back toward the input;\n",
        "- the feedforward sublayer bends the representation coordinatewise.\n",
        "\n",
        "Try increasing the attention mixing parameter and residual strength.  Notice that a Transformer layer is not simply a classifier or a lookup table; it is a learned geometric transformation of a whole sequence matrix.\n",
        "\n",
